{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd","id":1552079,"title":"Result based determinism (RBD)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-07-05T14:54:47-07:00","id":1552079,"title":"Result based determinism (RBD)","content":"In M20, we are presented two options to determine how an effect takes place and which spheres are needed.<br /> Process based determinism (PDB), which is the default one (I guess) and RBD.<br /> <br /> The first asks you *how* you want to change reality, while the last seems to ask &quot;what&quot; you want to do.<br /> <br /> There is the famous example of &quot;I want to go from point A to point B&quot;. While there are several ways of accomplishing that, in RBD the magr could &quot;summon&quot; a taxi and get all green signs and no traffic and simply go to point B. While in PBD this couldn't be done (you would need Matter, Life, Forces, etc etc etc to spawn a taxi and a driver). <br /> <br /> This only example does not explain much to me, so I present some questions:<br /> <br /> 1) Is RBD determined by the ST or the player has some say about it? I mean, the player just says &quot;I want to go from A to B&quot; and the ST chooses what happens or the player can choose how?<br /> <br /> 2) If the player chooses in the above question does this means that RBD is a PBD with more options?<br /> <br /> 3) Is RBD always coincident?<br /> <br /> 4) If the the player says &quot;I want to electrocute this guy&quot; what would be the differences in the processes of magick rules under PBD and RBD? Would the required spheres change? <br /> <br /> Feel free to shed some light on how RBD works!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-05T16:12:17-07:00","id":1552083,"title":"","content":"Part of the problem with addressing this, is that pairing with HAP/HOO matters a lot to how either RBD and PBD feel in play. Generally speaking, I've seen most people feel that PBD + HOO far too restrictive to actually get anything done, and RBD + HAP far too easy to avoid all the intended pitfalls of magic. So PBD tends to be paired with HAP (which is the default M20 sets out, and is fairly direct about, no need to guess), and RBD tends to be paired with HOO. Both have different feels to them, but by pairing the more restrictive of one category with the less restrict of the other category tends to mean you get something of a happy middle ground where magic can do things, but has some meaningful limits on it.<br /> <br /> But without that pairing made, it's hard to say things like how easy it is to make things coincidental with RBD.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-06T08:00:07-07:00","id":1552102,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552083#post1552083\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Part of the problem with addressing this, is that pairing with HAP/HOO matters a lot to how either RBD and PBD feel in play. Generally speaking, I've seen most people feel that PBD + HOO far too restrictive to actually get anything done, and RBD + HAP far too easy to avoid all the intended pitfalls of magic. So PBD tends to be paired with HAP (which is the default M20 sets out, and is fairly direct about, no need to guess), and RBD tends to be paired with HOO. Both have different feels to them, but by pairing the more restrictive of one category with the less restrict of the other category tends to mean you get something of a happy middle ground where magic can do things, but has some meaningful limits on it.<br /> <br /> But without that pairing made, it's hard to say things like how easy it is to make things coincidental with RBD.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough. I was considering HAP, because I always thought that HOO was unplayable. I mean, if the observer is omniscient, wouldn't he *always* know when it's magick doing the stuff? And so all magick is vulgar?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-06T12:24:34-07:00","id":1552111,"title":"","content":"So, omniscient is something I always felt was a bad word for how the earlier discussions on this intended. The HOO isn't a deity. They're still a human. The classic example is that the HOO can see inside your pocket that nothing was in there, and then suddenly something was in there, while the HAP cannot. The HOO can't perceive quantum mechanics in action, or see X-rays, or anything else beyond normal human sensory ranges (at least in its early incarnations, Internet discussions have dragged these terms around a lot as defenders and detractors have both equivocated in attempts to bolster their positions). The way it was intended originally was more a long the lines of: when magic is being cast, time pauses and a human with 5 in every Attribute and Ability is allow to fully search the entirety of everything being manipulated by the magic. So the HOO has limits, even if very weak ones.<br /> <br /> So, if you use magic to summon a business card into your pocket, the HOO would know and it would be vulgar (probably w/o witnesses, which really isn't as unplayable as people think unless you use a lot of on-the-fly magic in your games), because in that moment your pocket and the state of the business card is something they can observe. However, if you use magic so that reality warps such that the card was in your pocket since you added it in on your way out the house, the HOO would see that you put a business card in your pocket and carried it around until you had a reason to pull it out of your pocket.<br /> <br /> If you're running PBD, that's a ridiculously difficult feat of magic. But RBD generally makes it a far more tenable option, because the result of &quot;I have a card in my pocket&quot; is all you need to account for mechanically. This is, in effect, both why some people love RBD + HOO, and some people hate it. For some the fun of the game is coming up with ways to trick the HOO despite the &quot;omniscience&quot; in how you describe the process since the mechanics only care about the results. For others, this is entirely unfun because it becomes all about the player's skills and knowledge rather than their character's abilities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-06T14:35:29-07:00","id":1552114,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552111#post1552111\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, omniscient is something I always felt was a bad word for how the earlier discussions on this intended. The HOO isn't a deity. They're still a human. The classic example is that the HOO can see inside your pocket that nothing was in there, and then suddenly something was in there, while the HAP cannot. The HOO can't perceive quantum mechanics in action, or see X-rays, or anything else beyond normal human sensory ranges (at least in its early incarnations, Internet discussions have dragged these terms around a lot as defenders and detractors have both equivocated in attempts to bolster their positions). The way it was intended originally was more a long the lines of: when magic is being cast, time pauses and a human with 5 in every Attribute and Ability is allow to fully search the entirety of everything being manipulated by the magic. So the HOO has limits, even if very weak ones.</div> </div> </div> </div>I see. This is not clear at all, at least in M20.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552111#post1552111\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, if you use magic to summon a business card into your pocket, the HOO would know and it would be vulgar (probably w/o witnesses, which really isn't as unplayable as people think unless you use a lot of on-the-fly magic in your games), because in that moment your pocket and the state of the business card is something they can observe. However, if you use magic so that reality warps such that the card was in your pocket since you added it in on your way out the house, the HOO would see that you put a business card in your pocket and carried it around until you had a reason to pull it out of your pocket.<br /> <br /> If you're running PBD, that's a ridiculously difficult feat of magic. But RBD generally makes it a far more tenable option, because the result of &quot;I have a card in my pocket&quot; is all you need to account for mechanically. This is, in effect, both why some people love RBD + HOO, and some people hate it. For some the fun of the game is coming up with ways to trick the HOO despite the &quot;omniscience&quot; in how you describe the process since the mechanics only care about the results. For others, this is entirely unfun because it becomes all about the player's skills and knowledge rather than their character's abilities.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, that definitely explains a lot about the options. So, based on that, what would you say that the answers for the OP questions would be?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-06T17:54:53-07:00","id":1552126,"title":"","content":"So, presuming my take on HOO:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd#post1552079\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) Is RBD determined by the ST or the player has some say about it? I mean, the player just says &quot;I want to go from A to B&quot; and the ST chooses what happens or the player can choose how?</div> </div> </div> </div>There's two different parts here.<br /> <br /> What Spheres are needed is ultimately up to the ST. &quot;I want to go from A to B&quot; can be accomplished by a few Spheres (esp. with RBD), but Forces 1 isn't enough to fly you there if you want to use Forces to do it.<br /> <br /> The description of what happens in RBD is generally handled by the player with ST oversight; usually based on the Spheres used.<br /> <br /> That isn't some sort of sneaky PBD stuff, but more like &quot;you can't add in doing damage by having your driver run someone over if you're just using Correspondence to travel quickly and you're describing it as a taxi just happening to be there and the lights being in your favor.&quot; The results still have to be factored in, and doing damage is adding results to the goal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2) If the player chooses in the above question does this means that RBD is a PBD with more options? </div> </div> </div>Not... really? RBD vs. PBD is which Spheres are necessary for an effect. The player agency is largely placed in how the effect is described and thus how things like Coincidence vs. Vulgar is assigned.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3) Is RBD always coincident? </div> </div> </div>No, but it can feel that way depending on the ST and players. Since the players are granted wide leniency to describe how the effect plays out, it's much easier to keep effects coincidental, but is ultimately a player skill mediated interaction. Even then, some results can't simply get explained away. If I have my mage turn themselves into a kaiju, it's going to be vulgar because kaiju don't exist and there's no coincidental way to explain away how a human can turn into one.<br /> <br /> As well, Reality Zones are also going to impact things in this arena. What might pass in the general Consensus ruled normal reality isn't necessarily going to work in a Technocratic lab. VAs and SoEs might have enough training as former Technocratic groups to know how to pass off magic in a Technocratic Reality Zone, but most Tradition mages don't know the inner workings of Technocracy paradigms well enough to know how to explain lots of things as coincidental that would fly in other places.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4) If the the player says &quot;I want to electrocute this guy&quot; what would be the differences in the processes of magick rules under PBD and RBD? Would the required spheres change? </div> </div> </div>The biggest difference here would be if there's a nearby source of sufficient electricity to take advantage of. If you're out in the middle of nowhere on a clear day, PBD and RBD both agree that zapping someone with lightning conjured from nothingness is a Forces 3/Prime 2 effect. RBD gets the edge once there's high levels of electricity around to take advantage of. If there's a nearby power line, in RBD it would be relatively easy (Forces 2, maybe a few other options) to zap someone by bringing the wire down on them (Forces 2 allows you to influence existing Forces as attacks without the Forces 3/Prime 2 making a fireball from nothing approach). PBD could still potentially skip Prime, but would increase the number of successes needed (and possibly need to add Matter ) because they need to account for actually ripping the wire down and aiming it at the target.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-07T06:45:47-07:00","id":1552139,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552126#post1552126\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not... really? RBD vs. PBD is which Spheres are necessary for an effect. The player agency is largely placed in how the effect is described and thus how things like Coincidence vs. Vulgar is assigned.</div> </div> </div> </div>I thought RBD vs PBD was also what actually happens (besides which spheres are necessary). What I mean is that under PBD you can only go from A to B using Correspondence by teleporting, while with RBD you can teleport or take some sort of transport. And that's the player choice, right?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552126#post1552126\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The biggest difference here would be if there's a nearby source of sufficient electricity to take advantage of. If you're out in the middle of nowhere on a clear day, PBD and RBD both agree that zapping someone with lightning conjured from nothingness is a Forces 3/Prime 2 effect. RBD gets the edge once there's high levels of electricity around to take advantage of. If there's a nearby power line, in RBD it would be relatively easy (Forces 2, maybe a few other options) to zap someone by bringing the wire down on them (Forces 2 allows you to influence existing Forces as attacks without the Forces 3/Prime 2 making a fireball from nothing approach). PBD could still potentially skip Prime, but would increase the number of successes needed (and possibly need to add Matter ) because they need to account for actually ripping the wire down and aiming it at the target.</div> </div> </div> </div>Doesn't that mean that RBD is like PBD with more options? Like, with RBD you can try and make it coincident with all sort of explantions and if you can't you just go full vulgar, just like PBD, but RBD allows more explanations than PBD...<br /> <br /> Let's try a few more examples:<br /> <br /> - The mage wants to use Life to make someone sleepy: That is probably coincident in both PBD and RBD. Also would only require Life, but under RBD the target could &quot;just happen to be sleepy&quot;, or &quot;took too much chamomile tea earlier&quot;, while under PBD you would have to actively do something to the target system. Is that correct?<br /> This also raises another question, wouldn't RBD be like a constant war between player and ST. That increase in player agency means the ST could (or would have to..) say &quot;No, the guard didnt drink anything that made him sleepy&quot;, or else the players take over the story?<br /> <br /> - The mage wants to shapechange into a dog: Both PBD and RBD says it's vulgar and needs Life, so they're the same<br /> <br /> -The mage wants to get invisible and walk past the guard: Under PBD you can get literally invisible with vulgar magick and just walk past, or try to use shadows or other distractions to avoid detection, but this would only mean that you get some stealth bonus (or the target gets perception penalties). While under RBD you could just say the shadows and distractions happened and the mage walked past the guard, no additional roll or penalties needed. Both would require Forces 2. Is everything here correct?<br /> <br /> If my analysis is correct, notice that RBD can do anything PBD can and more...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-07T08:09:28-07:00","id":1552145,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The mage wants to use Life to make someone sleepy: That is probably coincident in both PBD and RBD. Also would only require Life, but under RBD the target could &quot;just happen to be sleepy&quot;, or &quot;took too much chamomile tea earlier&quot;, while under PBD you would have to actively do something to the target system. Is that correct?<br /> This also raises another question, wouldn't RBD be like a constant war between player and ST. That increase in player agency means the ST could (or would have to..) say &quot;No, the guard didnt drink anything that made him sleepy&quot;, or else the players take over the story?</div> </div> </div> </div>This reminds me of the Book of Shadows, 1e player handbook (and the last one, I think?). RDB was much more present in the books back then, compared to Revised.<br /> <br /> One thing that stuck with me was the idea that mages, unlike other splats, had quasi-narrative powers due to how vague are the limits. That the players of Mage were sort of STing too, in a sense. That section adviced players to show restrain and not try to destroy the ST story, but rather colaborate to make a better story even if that's not always good for the character<br /> <br /> So, I think this increased agency it's as Mage was intended to be played - at least at some point.<br /> <br /> Which doesn't mean there weren't figths. I would imagine that these were very frecuent, especialy with how &quot;adversarial&quot; STing was so common back then. Maybe that's why authors choosed to follow a different path.<br /> <br /> With how narrative systems have evolved over the last years, I think it could be cool to see how a &quot;revised RDB&quot;, doubling down on the original Mage ideas, could play out (like, a &quot;play Mage with RDB and don't die trying&quot; manual, explaining the best practices of RDB, such as how mix it with HOO, providing guidelines, maybe rules, to help players towards a smoother narrative experience).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 07-07-2025, 11:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-07T11:26:14-07:00","id":1552154,"title":"","content":"I've considered the possibility of mixing PBD and RBD in the same game, using the former to represent mages who view Magick as an impersonal force that the mage harnesses, while the latter is used to represent mages who view Magick as something willful that the mage interacts with. Examples of the latter might be an animist, a priest, or the magician in <i>The Last Unicorn</i> (&quot;Magick, so as you will&quot;) \u2014 or, more generally, a Mage counterpart to Changeling Unleashings. <br /> <br /> In setting, this would be represented by a different relationship between the Mage and the Avatar: with PBD, the Avatar restricts itself solely to the role of advisor or guide, and doesn't participate in the actual working of Magick beyond enabling the Mage to work it. With RBD, the Avatar serves as an assistant when working Magick, determining the specifics of how the Magick works after the mage sets out the general guidelines. OOC, the player can still suggest the details of how the Magick works, but the final call gets left to the Storyteller. <br /> <br /> But that raises the question of whether it should always be HAB, or if the RBD mages should also operate under HOO.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-07T13:38:19-07:00","id":1552163,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552154#post1552154\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've considered the possibility of mixing PBD and RBD in the same game, using the former to represent mages who view Magick as an impersonal force that the mage harnesses, while the latter is used to represent mages who view Magick as something willful that the mage interacts with. Examples of the latter might be an animist, a priest, or the magician in <i>The Last Unicorn</i> (&quot;Magick, so as you will&quot;) \u2014 or, more generally, a Mage counterpart to Changeling Unleashings.<br /> <br /> In setting, this would be represented by a different relationship between the Mage and the Avatar: with PBD, the Avatar restricts itself solely to the role of advisor or guide, and doesn't participate in the actual working of Magick beyond enabling the Mage to work it. With RBD, the Avatar serves as an assistant when working Magick, determining the specifics of how the Magick works after the mage sets out the general guidelines. OOC, the player can still suggest the details of how the Magick works, but the final call gets left to the Storyteller.<br /> <br /> But that raises the question of whether it should always be HAB, or if the RBD mages should also operate under HOO.</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1683\" data-vbnamecard=\"1683\">Aleph</a><br /> <br /> RBD also reminds me of how magick is supposed to work irl occultism. The magician often visualize/states/etc the result and leaves the options on how to get them for the universe. I think it's kinda cool to have this into the game, but *all* magick being like that seems kinda lame, imho\u2026<br /> <br /> Dataweaver, I guess the best option would be to allow both systems at the same time, depending if the mage focus on each step of the effect he wants to manifest, ot if he wants to just leave it for fate. I can also see Avatars being involved like you said, just not in a static way (I mean, the mage could choose how it would run).<br /> <br /> Mechanically, I'm pretty clueless on how this would affect the game (as I never used RBD) and also still can't see much difference between the effects except for some very specific cases.<br /> <br /> i would appreciate if you guys could help answer my questions or bring new examples to shed more light on the matter.<br /> <br /> Also, regarding HOO and HAP, I see no real reason to use HOO. I might be wrong, though.<br /> <br /> <br /> PS: Aleph, about the player's agency, I guess the best option is (as always) a balance, tending towards the ST of course, in the sense that the player proposes what he wants to happen (even adding stuff to the in game world) and the ST says if it's ok or not. Leaving the control to the ST can be disappointing and leaving too much to the player would be a mess lol.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3153,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","user":"Lord Revan","date":". 07-07-2025, 04:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-07T13:54:12-07:00","id":1552165,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552139#post1552139\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I thought RBD vs PBD was also what actually happens (besides which spheres are necessary). What I mean is that under PBD you can only go from A to B using Correspondence by teleporting, while with RBD you can teleport or take some sort of transport. And that's the player choice, right?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's always the player's choice on what the effect does. You can get a taxi and manipulate traffic to get you from A to B with PBD too; it just takes more Spheres to account for actually doing all of the steps (aka the process in PBD) instead of just having to determine the result in Sphere selection. The player always determines the intent of the effect; including what it's ideal results look like (qualified because of things like opting for a partial success). <br /> <br /> If you have a Mage with every Sphere at 5, and Arete 10, the difference between PBD and RBD is nearly meaningless (maybe a slight difference in base difficulty here or there, or the number of successes needed). At the maximum bounds, RBD cannot do more than PBD. RBD is however less restrictive for the average PC mage. In a sense, at average PC levels RBD can &quot;do more&quot; but only because it reduces the Sphere requirements on many effects significantly, and Spheres are expensive to raise so it takes a very long time (or starting with a lot of extra dots) to start to feel the gap close.<br /> <br /> RBD allows for more explanations <u>without increasing the mechanical burden on the effect</u>. The explanations are theoretically available to PBD as well, but the PBD approach means many explanations are effectively cut off because the mechanical burden gets too high for the PC in question.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The mage wants to use Life to make someone sleepy: That is probably coincident in both PBD and RBD. Also would only require Life, but under RBD the target could &quot;just happen to be sleepy&quot;, or &quot;took too much chamomile tea earlier&quot;, while under PBD you would have to actively do something to the target system. Is that correct? </div> </div> </div>No. They're equal in this case. And it's worth remembering that the rest of the game still exists beyond this axis of game-play modes. Rules like, &quot;your effect has to match your Focus,&quot; still matter. In either case you need to explain how the effect operates within your PC's Focus as well as what it looks like. Either mode is still &quot;I make them sleepy, and here's how,&quot; even if they might put more stress on some aspects of the explanations than others.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This also raises another question, wouldn't RBD be like a constant war between player and ST. </div> </div> </div>It can be. But it depends on the group. Even hardcore PBD + HOO proponents can run into this because Mage is a game with a high degree of player agency and powers that are designed to encourage creative application. Whichever interpretative approach a table takes, the dynamics of ST and player interactions is very quickly going to push towards an antagonistic or a cooperative approach because of the inherent basis of how the game works.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The mage wants to get invisible and walk past the guard: Under PBD you can get literally invisible with vulgar magick and just walk past, or try to use shadows or other distractions to avoid detection, but this would only mean that you get some stealth bonus (or the target gets perception penalties). While under RBD you could just say the shadows and distractions happened and the mage walked past the guard, no additional roll or penalties needed. Both would require Forces 2. Is everything here correct? </div> </div> </div>RBD doesn't change the mechanics. If you're running RBD + HOO, in this example the RBD would still result in a difficulty modifier because that's how the base mechanics model making yourself more stealthy by magically taking advantage of shadows and timing to move past the guard. Though I would argue either PBD or RBD could go the difficulty reduction route with a Rank 1 effect, and not just with Forces (Entropy - sense the weakness in the guards' patrol pattern - and Time - knowing the perfect timing to move before a guard or camera can turn around - are both plausible options). <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If my analysis is correct, notice that RBD can do anything PBD can and more... </div> </div> </div>One on hand, yes, RBD is the more permissive of the two options.<br /> <br /> That said, again I'll stress, there is the rest of the game to consider as a mitigating factor here. Running the game with RBD is still bound by what the Sphere levels say they can do, how the game describes what the outcome of magical effects are mechanically (dealing X damage, modifying difficulties, etc.), what the Feats of Magic success table says, what the duration table says, the Correspondence &quot;range&quot; table, etc., etc., etc.<br /> <br /> An Arete 1 mage under RBD might be able to attempt a bunch of effects that they might not even be allowed to roll for in PBD, but that doesn't mean they insta-succeed as if they rolled a bucket of successes. They're still limited by what the effects can do within the rest of the system, and they're still stuck at 1 success (or 2 with WP) for a single roll as the most they're going to get out of it.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-09T11:42:35-07:00","id":1552238,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552165#post1552165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's always the player's choice on what the effect does.<br /> (\u2026)</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with you, adding a couple observations. The mage would need knowledge about many subjects: in the taxi example, how to create a car and a human being.<br /> Also, the way things manifest would be different in PBD vs RBD as the taxi would be &quot;magickal&quot; (and thus could be unweaved, right?), while in RBD the only magickal aspect is that you convinced the universe to make that mundane taxi appear and help you. (When I think nore about RBD, it kinda seems cool).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552165#post1552165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No. They're equal in this case. And it's worth remembering that the rest of the game still exists beyond this axis of game-play modes. Rules like, &quot;your effect has to match your Focus,&quot; still matter. In either case you need to explain how the effect operates within your PC's Focus as well as what it looks like. Either mode is still &quot;I make them sleepy, and here's how,&quot; even if they might put more stress on some aspects of the explanations than others.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm. I know you're right, but I dont know how exactly that explanation would work without turning RBD into a PBD\u2026 I think that's because I don't quite grasp RBD yet.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552165#post1552165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">RBD doesn't change the mechanics. If you're running RBD + HOO, in this example the RBD would still result in a difficulty modifier because that's how the base mechanics model making yourself more stealthy by magically taking advantage of shadows and timing to move past the guard. Though I would argue either PBD or RBD could go the difficulty reduction route with a Rank 1 effect, and not just with Forces (Entropy - sense the weakness in the guards' patrol pattern - and Time - knowing the perfect timing to move before a guard or camera can turn around - are both plausible options).</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, then what would be the difference between PBD and RBD in this scenario?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552165#post1552165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One on hand, yes, RBD is the more permissive of the two options.<br /> <br /> That said, again I'll stress, there is the rest of the game to consider as a mitigating factor here. Running the game with RBD is still bound by what the Sphere levels say they can do, how the game describes what the outcome of magical effects are mechanically (dealing X damage, modifying difficulties, etc.), what the Feats of Magic success table says, what the duration table says, the Correspondence &quot;range&quot; table, etc., etc., etc.<br /> <br /> An Arete 1 mage under RBD might be able to attempt a bunch of effects that they might not even be allowed to roll for in PBD, but that doesn't mean they insta-succeed as if they rolled a bucket of successes. They're still limited by what the effects can do within the rest of the system, and they're still stuck at 1 success (or 2 with WP) for a single roll as the most they're going to get out of it.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I understand. Could you provide a few examples where there are difference between PBD and RBD, so I can better understand RBD rules?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-10T11:23:13-07:00","id":1552268,"title":"","content":"I've always thought summoning a taxi would be some kind of Corr 3 Mind 3 (or is it Mind 4) effect to effectively &quot;throw a net&quot; magically speaking to find a nearby Taxi and compel them to come to you. Still very coincidental from a RBD or PBD in my opinion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2783-tytalus","name":"Tytalus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2783_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"491"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-10T12:45:54-07:00","id":1552271,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552238#post1552238\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I understand. Could you provide a few examples where there are difference between PBD and RBD, so I can better understand RBD rules?</div> </div> </div> </div>So, I think the important thing is to stress that RBD vs. PBD is what Spheres you need to use more than anything else. Take everything else out. Ignore HAP vs. HOO because you're in a Sanctum. Assume you've got some decent stats compared to a starting PC but not the maxed out level of stuff.<br /> <br /> The most illustrative examples are when the player wants to do something that has a simple intention, but would be a lot of steps without magic.<br /> <br /> Basically, you're not going to figure this out with examples like &quot;trying to sneak past guards,&quot; because even a mortal in the WoD can just try to roll Stealth to get past guards. It's not complicated. Mages can just do it better.<br /> <br /> That's why the &quot;taxi&quot; example is kinda standard. Though part of the context of it that I feel got lost is the Seven Leagues Stride effect that started it. Seven League Stride is an older example Corr effect that was dropped pretty fast because of all of this causing debates. The basic point is that instead of teleporting (obviously vulgar) you could travel faster. This goes back to old Abrahamic faith traditions holding that prophets/saints could simply walk to where they were needed to be and arrive exactly when intended, neither late nor early (if you're getting LotR vibes... that's not accidental on Tolkien's part). There are apocryphal stories of such holy folk being seen just walking normally by people walking in the opposite direction, but they'd be spotted hours or even days worth of walking be travelers that couldn't have possibly seen the same person.<br /> <br /> The &quot;make this work for modern mages, and especially not religious mages,&quot; is what led to the &quot;taxi cab plus lots of luck with traffic&quot; effect. The debates started because on one hand &quot;just travel faster&quot; shouldn't require different Spheres for different paradigms/Focuses. If pulling a Seven League Stride is just Correspondence for mages pulling from those faith traditions, then it should also just be Correspondence for a modern gutter punk mage using a taxi cab. What we now condense into the PBD camp was usually arguing that this aren't actually the same effect. The saintly Chorister is creating and following a magical path that doesn't obey the laws of physics to get somewhere faster, so it's just Correspondence, while the taxi cab is moving like a normal taxi cab and the effects use different spheres because the actual effect is the manipulation of the city traffic systems to make the trip as short as possible. What we condense into the RBD camp took the opposite approach that there were the same effect just with different &quot;set dressings&quot; for different styles of magic.<br /> <br /> As for more examples... I'm not sure what you'd find more helpful. I don't generally play RBD. I'm doing my best to be even handed on why people do because I don't think they're wrong to play the game in a fashion they find fun; and you deserve a chance to understand why they do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-11T06:59:49-07:00","id":1552279,"title":"","content":"The point to understand is that RBD isn't &quot;being incredibly lucky&quot;. It <b>may be <i>appearing to be</i></b> incredibly lucky.<br /> <br /> In the taxi example, the taxi and the traffic still behave as they do because the Mage did Magic.<br /> <br /> You create the Magic exactly the same way you would do in PBD. Same factors, same requisites, same rolls. You still apply Instruments the same way, and you still must follow your Paradigm in the resulting effect.<br /> <br /> What changes is that instead of accounting for every aspect of the resulting spell, you only accounts for the result. In the taxi example, the result of the spell is to travel fast. However this travel happens, <i>as long as it doesn't give other benefits</i>, isn't important to determine Spheres, Successes, or the roll. It <b>is</b> important for Paradigm, Instruments and Coincidence, though.<br /> <br /> So, what happened? The taxi may be there by coincidence, <i>or even be an entirely magical construct</i>. The traffic may be really easy, or some shortcuts may actually <i>defy the laws of physics</i>, just not in an obvious way. The taxi isn't just a coincidence, because that would be an <b>Entropy</b> effect. The whole thing is, still, Magic. It's just that the Mage doesn't have to account for every detail when defining the spell's parameters.<br /> <br /> Focus on Spheres and number of successes. They're the main difference between RBD and PBD. It's just that PBD accounts for every detail in the <i>process</i> to define them, while RBD will only account for the result.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-11T08:44:51-07:00","id":1552283,"title":"","content":"Agreed 100% , the intent would be about what determines the Spheres needed. If possible the effects should remain the same, with minimal differences.<br /> <br /> Here's another example of RBD vs. PBD:<br /> <br /> Let's say a VA want's to scry on a distant location using his computer. So, he describes as haking into cammeras to display the far image into his PC.<br /> <br /> Scrying on a distant location it's Corr 2, but a PDB ST would argue you <i>also</i> need Forces 2 to either hack into the cammeras (if the ST agrees in that there are cameras there in the first place), or to directly display the results of the Corr effect as a video in your computer (if the ST decides there aren't cammeras there, but still allows you to roll with it because &quot;magick&quot;).<br /> <br /> Neither it's, strictly speaking, a Vulgar effect, but on PBD you need an additional Sphere.<br /> <br /> A PBD ST could say in his defense that you can totally do something like this with Corr 2 alone ... if you instead open a spatial window and superpose it to your computer screen. The result being an image in your computer imples additional things that Corr doesn't cover, and could be exploited (i.e. you can save a video, edit it with software). <hr /><br /> One could think that RBD never it's Vulgar, because of how the taxi-cab example works - but that's not really the case. Even that example could be Vulgar if the distance traveled it's too far, and the travel too quick, to be plausible. Even the best car at max speed, with all the trafick playing to it, takes some time to go from A to B<br /> <br /> The reason why it &quot;reduces&quot; Vulgarity it's because RBD reduces the Spheres needed to do otherwsie complex effects to play with plausibility. A taxi it's already faster than someone on foot, so you can travel long distances faster w/o straining belief<br /> <br /> More so if the ST agrees it's plausible to skip some parts of the taxi's travel entirely w/o incurring Vulgarity (here's where the axis of HOO vs HAP enters, some HAP believes on &quot;the batman&quot; disappearing after jumping trough a window in the night, may as well miss a car if the conditions are right) <hr />RBD doesn't mesh well with players seeking exploits. But then, arguably, Mage doesn't mesh well with players seeking exploits.<br /> <br /> It does mesh really well with Paradigms that don't have easy explanation for things just happening, and rather relly on complex processes to do stuf. Such as the Syndicate<br /> <br /> In fact: If you want more good examples on RBD, I would dare say ... Search stuff about for how the Syndicate does magick. Even on M20.<br /> <br /> Like how they summon stuff via calling a helicopter or drone to drop the stuff near them.<br /> <br /> Supposedly M20 doesn't use RBD, but the Syndicate kinda does <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 07-11-2025, 11:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-11T08:58:58-07:00","id":1552284,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let's say a VA want's to scry on a distant location using his computer. So, he describes as haking into cammeras to display the far image into his PC.<br /> <br /> Scrying on a distant location it's Corr 2, but a PDB ST would argue you <i>also</i> need Forces 2 to either hack into the cammeras (if the ST agrees in that there are cameras there in the first place), or to directly display the results of the Corr effect as a video in your computer (if the ST decides there aren't cammeras there, but still allows you to roll with it because &quot;magick&quot;).</div> </div> </div> </div>I said that this isn't Vulgar, and it surely isn't on HAP - but maybe a HOO ST wouldn't <i>allow </i>the effect as described if there aren't hidden cammeras. You may be able to do it anyway, but in a more Vulgar fashion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 07-11-2025, 12:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-11T10:10:18-07:00","id":1552285,"title":"","content":"In fact, the screening through the computer would be an interesting case of an effect being Vulgar in RBD, but Coincident in PBD, if we assume RBD + HOO and PBD + HAP, if there's no camera at the end.<br /> <br /> Under HAP, the lack of a camera isn't relevant, as long as the effect is Coincident from the POV of someone looking at the computer (they have no way to know if there's a camera or not). But under HOO, since the Magic can't create the camera in a plausible way, it's Vulgar regardless.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-13T17:06:00-07:00","id":1552340,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552271#post1552271\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So, I think the important thing is to stress that RBD vs. PBD is what Spheres you need to use more than anything else. Take everything else out. Ignore HAP vs. HOO because you're in a Sanctum. Assume you've got some decent stats compared to a starting PC but not the maxed out level of stuff.</div> </div> </div> </div>My understanding was that the way magick work would change too. For instance, by PBD, the mage is creating a taxi and a driver magickally, while in RBD they are not magickal.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> , nice explanation. I srill struggle to think about other examples where there would be a difference between RBD and PBD.<br /> <br /> &quot;Focus on Spheres and number of successes&quot; <br /> That is true if you are comparing the *exact* same effect in both ways (pbd vs rbd). But if you're comparing the results, they use the same spheres in a different way.<br /> <br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1683\" data-vbnamecard=\"1683\">Aleph</a> , If going too fast with the taxi would be vulgar, why not just open a portal and travel instantly? I do understand your logic though. Going from USA to Europe by car in a few hours is definitely vulgar.<br /> <br /> Could you post some examples of RBD in mystic paradigm and which doesn't invol e Correspondence nor Matter effects (because those are easily represented by &quot;luckily being always there&quot;.<br /> <br /> <br /> I just cant think of good examples, at all:<br /> <br /> If the mage wants to fly to the top of the golden gate bridge it would be both vulgar and require Forces 2 in both rbd and pbd.<br /> <br /> if the mage wants to open a portal to the umbra, it would be Spirit 4 and vulgar in both rbd and pbd<br /> <br /> If the mage wants to make someone fall in love with him that would be Mind 3 (or Life) and coincident in both pbd and rbd.<br /> <br /> So, besides Matter creation and Correspondence, how else they can differ?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-15T13:34:51-07:00","id":1552424,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552340#post1552340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If going too fast with the taxi would be vulgar, why not just open a portal and travel instantly? I do understand your logic though. Going from USA to Europe by car in a few hours is definitely vulgar.</div> </div> </div> </div>Some Paradigms don't jive to well with the idea of just making things happen. Both the Syndicate and NWO are often portrayed as using Corr to appear elsewhere by using &quot;secret hidden passages&quot;. Because that's more &quot;conspiracy&quot; than dissasembling and reasembling molecules on a teleporter<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Could you post some examples of RBD in mystic paradigm and which doesn't invol e Correspondence nor Matter effects (because those are easily represented by &quot;luckily being always there&quot;. </div> </div> </div>Mystic paradigms don't change that much, comparatively, because they can schew the process. When you wave hands and make things happen, there's not much distance between intent and the Spheres being used as intended. And masking magick into coincidences it's well backed into the system, typically can be done with just a little creativity.<br /> <br /> Using Forces to shoot a bolt of energy from a wire to some enemy would be Vulgar, using Forces to make a street wire casually fall on an enemy and electrocute them, is not. But in spite of lookin as two very different Effects, both are roughly the same Forces 2 Effect. At most a RBD vs. PBD debate could be about if the latter needs one extra success to cut the wire, and whether should be easier to dodge (but dodge magick rules on Mage are pretty bad, and a different issue)<br /> <br /> Ultimately, if Mage never took a stance on this for 20 years it's because both styles are useable, and only cause problems on edge cases. Moreover, trying to stick to one may end seeming nitpicky and/or exploitative.<br /> <br /> Like, a Mage reads the future on tarot cards. The Mage has Time 2. But it's that enough to pull the correct cards for the reading?. Probably most ST wouldn't mind, the cards are vague enough that you don't need specific ones, and the process it's probably going to get glossed over. Same if you use a pendulum to read auras and stuff, do you need Forces for it to move wierd?, In my experience most ST would just allow you to use your instrument, no questions asked<br /> <br /> <br /> That being said, I do remember a particular one:<br /> <br /> I don't remember if this was mentioned in a book or by a player, but an example of Life I remember was: A mage was injured, and the ST declared that just healing that with Life would be Vulgar ... so the mage narrated that he was sent to the hospital, and was treated there.<br /> <br /> Now, this one it's a bit weird. Insofar as it pertains to making things Coincident, it usually it's better to just say that &quot;your wounds look worse than they are&quot;. There are enough cases of people surviving impossible wounds to make this believable. I guess the player just didn't tought on that ... or maybe the mage was trully demolished, being &quot;better than you look&quot; ain't going to re-attach that arm you lost over there :P . IDK<br /> <br /> Also one could say it would need more Spheres even on RBD, if only because the mage changed location. But probably that wasn't relevant to the narrative, so it was glossed over.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 07-15-2025, 04:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-15T18:24:24-07:00","id":1552433,"title":"","content":"At the end of the day the taxi example is an edge case (Seven Leagues Stride with a Gutter Magic paradigm). The differences will crop up mostly in edge cases. At most, RBD will be used more frequently for Mages whose paradigms actually demand more complex processes, like the Syndicate, who sometimes sort of uses the entire society as an Instrument.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-15T18:36:44-07:00","id":1552434,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552283#post1552283\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Agreed 100% , the intent would be about what determines the Spheres needed. If possible the effects should remain the same, with minimal differences.<br /> <br /> Here's another example of RBD vs. PBD:<br /> <br /> Let's say a VA want's to scry on a distant location using his computer. So, he describes as haking into cammeras to display the far image into his PC.<br /> <br /> Scrying on a distant location it's Corr 2, but a PDB ST would argue you <i>also</i> need Forces 2 to either hack into the cammeras (if the ST agrees in that there are cameras there in the first place), or to directly display the results of the Corr effect as a video in your computer (if the ST decides there aren't cammeras there, but still allows you to roll with it because &quot;magick&quot;).<br /> <br /> Neither it's, strictly speaking, a Vulgar effect, but on PBD you need an additional Sphere.<br /> <br /> A PBD ST could say in his defense that you can totally do something like this with Corr 2 alone ... if you instead open a spatial window and superpose it to your computer screen. The result being an image in your computer imples additional things that Corr doesn't cover, and could be exploited (i.e. you can save a video, edit it with software). <hr /><br /> One could think that RBD never it's Vulgar, because of how the taxi-cab example works - but that's not really the case. Even that example could be Vulgar if the distance traveled it's too far, and the travel too quick, to be plausible. Even the best car at max speed, with all the trafick playing to it, takes some time to go from A to B<br /> <br /> The reason why it &quot;reduces&quot; Vulgarity it's because RBD reduces the Spheres needed to do otherwsie complex effects to play with plausibility. A taxi it's already faster than someone on foot, so you can travel long distances faster w/o straining belief<br /> <br /> More so if the ST agrees it's plausible to skip some parts of the taxi's travel entirely w/o incurring Vulgarity (here's where the axis of HOO vs HAP enters, some HAP believes on &quot;the batman&quot; disappearing after jumping trough a window in the night, may as well miss a car if the conditions are right) <hr />RBD doesn't mesh well with players seeking exploits. But then, arguably, Mage doesn't mesh well with players seeking exploits.<br /> <br /> It does mesh really well with Paradigms that don't have easy explanation for things just happening, and rather relly on complex processes to do stuf. Such as the Syndicate<br /> <br /> In fact: If you want more good examples on RBD, I would dare say ... Search stuff about for how the Syndicate does magick. Even on M20.<br /> <br /> Like how they summon stuff via calling a helicopter or drone to drop the stuff near them.<br /> <br /> Supposedly M20 doesn't use RBD, but the Syndicate kinda does <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>I would argue both the VA putting a camera feed on their computer and the Syndicate rep &quot;summoning&quot; via helicopter or drone isn't really doing RBD.<br /> <br /> They're just using their Instruments.<br /> <br /> Do you NEED Forces 2 to put the results of a Corr 2 effect on a screen? I argue no. That's what the software in the machine is for. Corr 2 is establishing the connection to the other location (with or without a camera at the other end). Whereas, presumably, the Virtual Adept has programs on their computer to interpret that feed and turn it into a window that pops up on the screen. Magick isn't needed for that part, because Computer is already a skill well worn into the Consensus at this point. It's the tool working as intended.<br /> <br /> Presumably, the Syndicate rep HAS helicopters or drones on standby, or because of their wealth have connections within an existing supply chain. Their use of Magick bypasses the normal particulars of how those supply chains operate, getting what they need to them slightly faster than it would on its own. They &quot;know the system&quot;, and can thus finesse it in a way that a normal &quot;user&quot; couldn't. Same thing with using Human Resources as an Instrument: the Syndic's agents and helpers do a great deal of things in a mundane way. But within the gaps between them, and due to their employer's Enlightened direction, they accomplish extraordinary things.<br /> <br /> It's probably worth underlining that PBD and RBD are not hard binaries with concrete lines of demarcation. They are fluid and permeable, based on the suspension of disbelief of the ST and players involved.<br /> <br /> It's also dependent on the Real Life technology involved. The classic Taxi example of using Corr alone would clearly be RBD if the Mage were summoning the taxi on the spot, from nowhere. Under PBD, they'd need all manner of Matter, Prime, and/or Life to justify the taxi appearing.<br /> <br /> But <i>nowadays</i>, a Gutter Mage (or VA, or Syndic, or whatever) could &quot;summon a taxi&quot; using an <b>APP</b>. Using Corr to find one, the app to get the driver to come there, maybe more Corr to get the driver there faster (&quot;huh, I made really good time. Thanks, GPS!&quot;), and then using the same &quot;Seven League Stride&quot; principle to get the Mage where they wanted to go. All part of the same continuous Effect.<br /> <br /> An Effect that seems RBD can become considerably more PBD, if the player puts more thought into how the Magick interplays with the Mundane. (Advances in technology beyond the early 90s also help immensely in this regard.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-15T20:10:11-07:00","id":1552437,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552424#post1552424\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't remember if this was mentioned in a book or by a player, but an example of Life I remember was: A mage was injured, and the ST declared that just healing that with Life would be Vulgar ... so the mage narrated that he was sent to the hospital, and was treated there.</div> </div> </div> </div>I liked this example, thanks. It looks a lot RBD, since the result (healing) is what matters. So you only need Life 2. It also shows how rbd can be made coincident easier.<br /> Now, they do seem to take more time to happen? If time is crucial at that moment in the game, going to the hosptial (or calling a cab) would probably take some precious time the player might not have. I guess this is a good price to pay for the easier coincidental effect and player agency over the world.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar6156_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6156\" data-vbnamecard=\"6156\">Bluecho</a> ok, if those are just instruments at play, then I'm confused again. Could you provide your take of rbd and pbd with examples, please?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-15T21:29:13-07:00","id":1552439,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552079-result-based-determinism-rbd?p=1552434#post1552434\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I would argue both the VA putting a camera feed on their computer and the Syndicate rep &quot;summoning&quot; via helicopter or drone isn't really doing RBD.<br /> <br /> They're just using their Instruments.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Not exactly.<br /> <br /> You can't Magic your Instrument into existence just because it's an Instrument, doing so would be a Magical effect of its own. The computer is an Instrument regardless, sure.<br /> <br /> The question, though, is if there is a camera or not at the observed point, and if this camera is being actively used. Is this camera an Instrument, an Effect or a target?<br /> <br /> All three options can be used, but they are distinct effects.<br /> <br /> If there's no camera, then the spell has to either create the camera or to ignore it altogether. The camera is <u><b>not</b></u> an Instrument, because it doesn't exist to begin with. In this case, both methods can go ahead anyways with just <b>Correspondence</b>, but under RBD + HOO the effect is Vulgar since there's no real camera that could account for the image (there's either a fake camera or no camera at all, it doesn't really matter except for the unlikely occurrence of someone witnessing the camera appearing). Under PBD creating the camera would require higher <b>Correspondence</b>, <b>Matter</b> and <b>Prime</b>, but ignoring it would be simpler, and Coincident under HAP, so it's completely unnecessary.<br /> <br /> Only if the camera exist it can be a target or an Instrument. Even then, for it to be an Instrument you would have to first connect to the camera in some way under PBD, at which point it's possibly easier to just use the camera instead of Magic, but doing Magic can still convey many circumstantial benefits. The camera as a target would require added Spheres under PBD, but possibly with added benefits. Under RBD using the camera as an Instrument wouldn't require anything but <b>Correspondence</b>.<br /> <br /> Union paradigms usually favor, in this case, using the camera as either target or Instrument, not simply making images out of nothing, and the <b>Data</b> Sphere specifically calls for this (you can't scry with <b>Data</b> if there's no equipment at the end point). So while ignoring the camera may be easier under PBD, it may not be an option.<br /> <br /> Syndicate characters depend a lot on the player and how much forethought they put into it. I completely agree that <i>preferably</i> all those things should be Instruments. The Syndicate specializes into making human work, training and management into Instruments. But in practice RBD is a simpler solution to let players make Syndicate characters without demanding the forethought they should technically have. It's a grey area, but useful to think about nonetheless.<br /> <br /> But on the computer being able to record the image regardless, sorry, but this is false under RAW. More than once the rules alude to the fact that imprinting information into a computer system requires <b>Forces</b>. If you use the computer just as an Instrument, then the image isn't in the system at all, it is a Magical effect, regardless if it is conveyed through the computer. Without <b>Forces</b> this information will never arrive at the internal workings of the computer and the machine can't process the image in any real way (although your effect can still be able to).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}